s C (2, 2\u221a3) to F (3, 0 )\n\nSlope of CF: (0 - 2\u221a3)/(3 - 2) = (-2\u221a3)/1 = -2\u221a3\n\nEquation: Using point C (2, 2\u221a3):\n\ny - 2\u221a3 = -2\u221a3(x - 2 )\n\n=> y = -2\u221a3 x + 4\u221a3 + 2\u221a3\n\n=> y = -2\u221a3 x + 6\u221a3\n\nNow, we need to find the intersection points of these lines to form triangle RQS. Let's find the intersections of AD & BE, BE & CF, and CF & AD.\n\nFirst intersection: AD and BE.\n\nAD: y = (3\u221a3/5)x\n\nBE: y = (-\u221a3/7)x + (4\u221a3)/7\n\nSet equal:\n\n(3\u221a3/5)x = (-\u221a3/7)x + (4\u221a3)/7\n\nMultiply both sides by 35 to eliminate denominators:\n\n35*(3\u221a3/5)x = 35*(-\u221a3/7)x + 35*(4\u221a3)/7\n\n=> 7*3\u221a3 x = 5*(-\u221a3)x + 5*4\u221a3\n\n=> 21\u221a3 x = -5\u221a3 x + 20\u221a3\n\nBring terms together:\n\n21\u221a3 x + 5\u221a3 x = 20\u221a3\n\n26\u221a3 x = 20\u221a3\n\nDivide both sides by \u221a3:\n\n26x = 20\n\nx = 20/26 = 10/13\n\nThen y = (3\u221a3/5)*(10/13) = (30\u221a3)/(65) = (6\u221a3)/13\n\nSo intersection point R is at (10/13, 6\u221a3/13 )\n\nNext, intersection of BE and CF.\n\nBE: y = (-\u221a3/7)x + 4\u221a3/7\n\nCF: y = -2\u221a3 x + 6\u221a3\n\nSet equal:\n\n(-\u221a3/7)x + 4\u221a3/7 = -2\u221a3 x + 6\u221a3\n\nMultiply both sides by 7 to eliminate denominators:\n\n(-\u221a3)x + 4\u221a3 = -14\u221a3 x + 42\u221a3\n\nBring all terms to left side:\n\n(-\u221a3)x + 4\u221a3 + 14\u221a3 x - 42\u221a3 = 0\n\n(13\u221a3)x - 38\u221a3 = 0\n\nDivide both sides by \u221a3:\n\n13x - 38 = 0 => x = 38/13\n\nThen y = -2\u221a3*(38/13) + 6\u221a3 = (-76\u221a3)/13 + (78\u221a3)/13 = (2\u221a3)/13\n\nSo intersection point Q is at (38/13, 2\u221a3/13 )\n\nThird intersection: CF and AD.\n\nAD: y = (3\u221a3/5)x\n\nCF: y = -2\u221a3 x + 6\u221a3\n\nSet equal:\n\n(3\u221a3/5)x = -2\u221a3 x + 6\u221a3\n\nMultiply both sides by 5:\n\n3\u221a3 x = -10\u221a3 x + 30\u221a3\n\n13\u221a3 x = 30\u221a3\n\nx = 30/13\n\nThen y = (3\u221a3/5)*(30/13) = (90\u221a3)/65 = (18\u221a3)/13\n\nSo intersection point S is at (30/13, 18\u221a3/13 )\n\nTherefore, triangle RQS has vertices R(10/13, 6\u221a3/13), Q(38/13, 2\u221a3/13), and S(30/13, 18\u221a3/13).\n\nWait, but need to confirm which intersections correspond to which triangle. But since R is intersection of AD & BE, Q is BE & CF, S is CF & AD. So triangle RQS is formed by these three points.\n\nNow, the next step is to analyze the distances x, y, z from a point P inside RQS to the sides of ABC.\n\nIn the original triangle ABC, which is equilateral with side length 4. The distances from any interior point to the three sides are related by the formula x + y + z = height of the triangle.\n\nWait, in an equilateral triangle, the sum of the distances from any interior point to the three sides is equal to the height of the triangle.\n\nHeight h = (sqrt(3)/2)*side length = (sqrt(3)/2)*4 = 2 sqrt(3). Therefore, x + y + z = 2 sqrt(3) for any point inside ABC.\n\nBut in our case, point P is within triangle RQS, which is inside ABC. So even for points in RQS, x + y + z = 2 sqrt(3). So the sum is constant, but the product xyz varies.\n\nWe need to find the minimum of xyz when P is in RQS, and show that the minimum occurs at a vertex.\n\nThis seems similar to optimization under a constraint, where the constraint is x + y + z = 2 sqrt(3), and we need to minimize xyz. However, since the point is constrained to lie within RQS, which is a specific triangle inside ABC, we can't just apply the AM-GM inequality directly because x, y, z are not independent variables; their possible values are constrained by the position within RQS.\n\nBut the problem states that the minimum occurs at a vertex of RQS. So perhaps within RQS, the product xyz is minimized at one of its vertices. So first, I need to compute xyz at each vertex R, Q, S, and then show that for any point inside RQS, the product is at least the minimum of those three values.\n\nAlternatively, maybe there's a way to parametrize the coordinates of P in RQS and express x,