spectively.\" Since ABC is a triangle with three sides, the sides can be labeled as BC, AC, AB. So maybe the order is BC, AC, AB? But not sure.\n\nBut given that in coordinate terms, we can compute the three distances as follows:\n\nFor any point (p, q):\n\n- Distance to AB (y=0): z = q\n\n- Distance to BC (y = -\u221a3 x + 4\u221a3 ): x = | -\u221a3 p - q + 4\u221a3 | / 2\n\n- Distance to AC (y = \u221a3 x ): y = | \u221a3 p - q | / 2\n\nBut since the point P is inside ABC, the distances would be positive and the expressions inside the absolute value would be positive. Let me check.\n\nFor example, take point A(0,0):\n\n- Distance to BC: | -\u221a3*0 - 0 + 4\u221a3 | / 2 = 4\u221a3 / 2 = 2\u221a3\n\n- Distance to AC: | \u221a3*0 - 0 | / 2 = 0\n\n- Distance to AB: 0\n\nBut in reality, point A is on AB and AC, so distances to AB and AC are zero, and distance to BC is the height, which is 2\u221a3. So yes, the formulas hold.\n\nTherefore, for a general point inside ABC, the distances x, y, z to BC, AC, AB respectively are:\n\nx = ( -\u221a3 p - q + 4\u221a3 ) / 2\n\ny = ( \u221a3 p - q ) / 2\n\nz = q\n\nBut since the point is inside ABC, these expressions should be positive. Let's verify with point R(10/13, 6\u221a3/13):\n\nCompute x: ( -\u221a3*(10/13) - (6\u221a3/13) + 4\u221a3 ) / 2\n\n= ( (-10\u221a3 - 6\u221a3)/13 + 4\u221a3 ) / 2\n\n= ( (-16\u221a3)/13 + 52\u221a3/13 ) / 2\n\n= (36\u221a3/13)/2 = 18\u221a3/13\n\ny: ( \u221a3*(10/13) - 6\u221a3/13 ) / 2\n\n= (10\u221a3 - 6\u221a3)/13 / 2\n\n= (4\u221a3)/13 / 2 = 2\u221a3/13\n\nz: 6\u221a3/13\n\nSo x = 18\u221a3/13, y = 2\u221a3/13, z = 6\u221a3/13\n\nCheck x + y + z = (18\u221a3 + 2\u221a3 + 6\u221a3)/13 = 26\u221a3/13 = 2\u221a3, which matches the height. So the formulas are correct.\n\nSimilarly, for point Q(38/13, 2\u221a3/13):\n\nx: ( -\u221a3*(38/13) - (2\u221a3/13) + 4\u221a3 ) / 2\n\n= ( -38\u221a3 - 2\u221a3 + 52\u221a3 ) / 13 / 2\n\n= (12\u221a3)/13 / 2 = 6\u221a3/13\n\ny: ( \u221a3*(38/13) - 2\u221a3/13 ) / 2\n\n= (38\u221a3 - 2\u221a3)/13 / 2\n\n= (36\u221a3)/13 / 2 = 18\u221a3/13\n\nz: 2\u221a3/13\n\nAgain, x + y + z = (6\u221a3 + 18\u221a3 + 2\u221a3)/13 = 26\u221a3/13 = 2\u221a3\n\nSimilarly, for point S(30/13, 18\u221a3/13):\n\nx: ( -\u221a3*(30/13) - (18\u221a3/13) + 4\u221a3 ) / 2\n\n= (-30\u221a3 - 18\u221a3 + 52\u221a3)/13 / 2\n\n= (4\u221a3)/13 / 2 = 2\u221a3/13\n\ny: ( \u221a3*(30/13) - 18\u221a3/13 ) / 2\n\n= (30\u221a3 - 18\u221a3)/13 / 2\n\n= 12\u221a3/13 / 2 = 6\u221a3/13\n\nz: 18\u221a3/13\n\nSum: 2\u221a3/13 + 6\u221a3/13 + 18\u221a3/13 = 26\u221a3/13 = 2\u221a3\n\nGood, so the distances at each vertex R, Q, S are:\n\n- R: x = 18\u221a3/13, y = 2\u221a3/13, z = 6\u221a3/13. Product: (18\u221a3/13)*(2\u221a3/13)*(6\u221a3/13)\n\nCompute this: 18*2*6*(\u221a3)^3 / 13^3\n\n= 216*(3\u221a3) / 2197\n\n= 648\u221a3 / 2197\n\nSimilarly for Q:\n\nx = 6\u221a3/13, y = 18\u221a3/13, z = 2\u221a3/13. Product same as R:\n\n6*18*2*(\u221a3)^3 /13^3 = 216*3\u221a3 /2197 = 648\u221a3 /2197\n\nFor S:\n\nx = 2\u221a3/13, y = 6\u221a3/13, z = 18\u221a3/13. Product same as others: 648\u221a3 /2197\n\nSo all three vertices of RQS have the same product xyz. So the product is the same at each vertex. Therefore, if we can show that the minimum occurs at a vertex, then this product would be the minimum.\n\nBut the problem says to prove that when P is at a vertex of RQS, the product xyz achieves its minimum. So perhaps within the triangle RQS, the product xyz is minimized at the vertices.\n\nTo prove this, maybe we can use the method of Lagrange multipliers, considering the function f(x, y, z) = xyz with the constraint x + y + z = 2\u221a3, but also considering the linear constraints that define the triangle RQS. However, this might be complicated.\n\nAlternatively, since RQS is a convex polygon, and the function xyz is a smooth function, the extrema should occur either at critical points inside the triangle or on the boundary. If we can show that there are no critical points inside RQS, then the minimum must occur on the boundary. Then, on each edge, the minimum would occur at the endpoints, hence the vertices.\n\nBut first, let's check if there are any critical points inside RQS where the product xyz could be lower.\n\nLet me parametrize the problem. Since x + y + z = 2\u221a3, we can express z = 2\u221a3 - x - y, so the product becomes xyz = x y (2\u221a3 - x - y ). To find critical points, take partial derivatives with respect to x and y and set them to zero.\n\nCompute \u2202(xyz)/\u2202x = y(2\u221a3 - x - y ) + x y (-1) = y(2\u221a3 - x - y - x ) = y(2\u221a3 - 2x - y )\n\nSimilarly, \u2202(xyz)/\u2202y = x(2\u221a3 - x - y ) + x y (-1) = x(2\u221a3 - x - y - y ) = x(2\u221a3 - x - 2y )\n\nSet these equal to zero:\n\n1. y(2\u221a3 - 2x - y ) = 0\n\n2. x(2\u221a3 - x - 2y ) = 0\n\nAssuming x and y are positive (since inside the triangle), so y \u2260 0, x \u2260 0. Therefore:\n\n1. 2\u221a3 - 2x - y = 0\n\n2. 2\u221a3 - x - 2y = 0\n\nSo we have the system:\n\n2x + y = 2\u221a3\n\nx + 2y = 2\u221a3\n\nSolve this system:\n\nFrom first equation: y = 2\u221a3 - 2x\n\nSubstitute into second equation:\n\nx + 2(2\u221a3 - 2x ) = 2\u221a3\n\nx + 4\u221a3 - 4x = 2\u221a3\n\n-3x + 4\u221a3 = 2\u221a3\n\n-3x = -2\u221a3\n\nx = (2\u221a3)/3\n\nThen y = 2\u221a3 - 2*(2\u221a3)/3 = 2\u221a3 - (4\u221a3)/3 = (6\u221a3 - 4\u221a3)/3 = (2\u221a3)/3\n\nTherefore, critical point at x = y = (2\u221a3)/3, z = 2\u221a3 - x - y = 2\u221a3 - (4\u221a3)/3 = (6\u221a3 - 4\u221a3)/3 = (2\u221a3)/3\n\nSo the critical point is at x = y = z = (2\u221a3)/3, which is the centroid of the original triangle ABC. The product here is ((2\u221a3)/3)^3 = (8*3\u221a3)/27 = (24\u221a3)/27 = (8\u221a3)/9 \u2248 1.5396\n\nBut wait, in our vertices R, Q, S, the product was 648\u221a3 / 2197 \u2248 (648*1.732)/2197 \u2248 1122.3 / 2197 \u2248 0.510. Which is smaller than (8\u221a3)/9 \u2248 1.5396. So the centroid gives a higher product. Therefore, the critical point found is a local maximum, not a minimum.\n\nTherefore, the minimum must occur on the boundary of RQS. Then, on each edge of RQS, the minimum of xyz would occur at one of the endpoints. Hence, the overall minimum is at one of the vertices.\n\nBut we need to verify this.\n\nAlternatively, since RQS is a triangle inside ABC, and the product xyz is a multiplicative function. Since the product is minimized when at least one of the distances is minimized. However, since x + y + z is constant, the product is minimized when one variable is as small as possible, which might occur at a vertex where one of the distances is minimized.\n\nLooking at the vertices R, Q, S:\n\nAt R: y = 2\u221a3/13 \u2248 0.266\n\nAt Q: z = 2\u221a3/13 \u2248 0.266\n\nAt S: x = 2\u221a3/13 \u2248 0.266\n\nSo each vertex has one distance being 2\u221a3/13, and the other two distances being larger. Since the product is xyz, having one small term would make the product small. If all three distances are balanced, like at the centroid, the product is larger.\n\nTherefore, the minimal product occurs when one distance is minimized, which happens at the vertices. But we need to confirm that indeed at the vertices, one distance is minimized compared to other points in RQS.\n\nAlternatively, we can parametrize the edges of RQS and find the minimum on each edge.\n\nLet me consider edge RQ, from R(10/13, 6\u221a3/13) to Q(38/13, 2\u221a3/13). Let's parametrize this edge.\n\nParametric equations:\n\nx(t) = 10/13 + t*(38/13 - 10/13) = 10/13 + t*(28/13), t \u2208 [0,1]\n\ny(t) = 6\u221a3/13 + t*(2\u221a3/13 - 6\u221a3/13) = 6\u221a3/13 - 4\u221a3 t /13\n\nThen z(t) = y-coordinate, since z = q. Wait, no. Wait, in our coordinate system, z is the distance to AB, which is the y-coordinate. Wait, no:\n\nWait, earlier, we determined that:\n\nx = distance to BC = ( -\u221a3 p - q + 4\u221a3 ) / 2\n\ny = distance to AC = ( \u221a3 p - q ) / 2\n\nz = distance to AB = q\n\nSo in terms of coordinates (p, q):\n\nx = ( -\u221a3 p - q + 4\u221a3 ) / 2\n\ny = ( \u221a3 p - q ) / 2\n\nz = q\n\nTherefore, on edge RQ, parametrized as (10/13 + 28t/13, 6\u221a3/13 - 4\u221a3 t /13 ), t \u2208 [0,1]\n\nSo express x, y, z in terms of t:\n\nx(t) = [ -\u221a3*(10/13 + 28t/13 ) - (6\u221a3/13 - 4\u221a3 t /13 ) + 4\u221a3 ] / 2\n\nLet me compute numerator:\n\n-\u221a3*(10 + 28t)/13 - (6\u221a3 - 4\u221a3 t)/13 + 4\u221a3\n\n= [ -10\u221a3 - 28\u221a3 t -6\u221a3 + 4\u221a3 t ] /13 + 4\u221a3\n\n= [ -16\u221a3 -24\u221a3 t ] /13 + 4\u221a3\n\n= -16\u221a3/13 -24\u221a3 t /13 + 52\u221a3/13\n\n= (36\u221a3 -24\u221a3 t ) /13\n\nTherefore, x(t) = (36\u221a3 -24\u221a3 t ) / (13*2 ) = (18\u221a3 -12\u221a3 t ) /13 = 6\u221a3(3 - 2t)/13\n\nSimilarly, y(t) = [ \u221a3*(10/13 +28t/13 ) - (6\u221a3/13 -4\u221a3 t /13 ) ] /2\n\nCompute numerator:\n\n\u221a3*(10 +28t ) /13 -6\u221a3 +4\u221a3 t /13\n\n= [10\u221a3 +28\u221a3 t -6\u221a3 +4\u221a3 t ] /13\n\n= (4\u221a3 +32\u221a3 t ) /13\n\nTherefore, y(t) = (4\u221a3 +32\u221a3 t ) / (13*2 ) = (2\u221a3 +16\u221a3 t ) /13 = 2\u221a3(1 +8t ) /13\n\nz(t) = q(t) = 6\u221a3/13 -4\u221a3 t /13 = (6\u221a3 -4\u221a3 t ) /13 = 2\u221a3(3 - 2t ) /13\n\nThus, x(t) = 6\u221a3(3 - 2t)/13, y(t)=2\u221a3(1 +8t)/13, z(t)=2\u221a3(3 -2t)/13\n\nThe product x(t)y(t)z(t) is:\n\n[6\u221a3(3 - 2t)/13] * [2\u221a3(1 +8t)/13] * [2\u221a3(3 -2t)/13 ]\n\n= 6*2*2*(\u221a3)^3*(3 -2t)*(1 +8t)*(3 -2t)/13^3\n\n= 24*(3\u221a3)*(3 -2t)^2*(1 +8t)/2197\n\n= 72\u221a3*(3 -2t)^2*(1 +8t)/2197\n\nTo find the minimum of this expression for t \u2208 [0,1]\n\nLet\u2019s analyze the function f(t) = (3 -2t)^2*(1 +8t) on t \u2208 [0,1]\n\nExpand f(t):\n\nLet u = 3 -2t, then u goes from 3 - 0 = 3 to 3 -2*1 =1 as t goes from 0 to1.\n\nf(t) = u^2*(1 +8t ) = u^2*(1 +8*( (3 - u)/2 )) = ?\n\nWait, maybe it's better to just expand it.\n\n(3 -2t)^2 = 9 -12t +4t^2\n\nMultiply by (1 +8t ):\n\n= 9*(1 +8t ) -12t*(1 +8t ) +4t^2*(1 +8t )\n\n=9 +72t -12t -96t^2 +4t^2 +32t^3\n\n=9 +60t -92t^2 +32t^3\n\nSo f(t) =32t^3 