\vec{A}\\) remains \\(\\vec{A}\\)\n- \\(\\vec{B}\\) remains \\(\\vec{B}\\)\n- \\(\\vec{O}\\) is just the position vector of the circumcenter from C.\n\nThen, the vectors in the equation become:\n\n- \\(\\overrightarrow{CO} = \\vec{O} - \\vec{C} = \\vec{O}\\)\n- \\(\\overrightarrow{CA} = \\vec{A} - \\vec{C} = \\vec{A}\\)\n- \\(\\overrightarrow{CB} = \\vec{B} - \\vec{C} = \\vec{B}\\)\n\nSo substituting into the given equation:\n\n\\[\n\\vec{O} = t \\vec{A} + \\left(\\frac{1}{2} - \\frac{3t}{4}\\right) \\vec{B}\n\\]\n\nOkay, so the circumcenter O is expressed as a linear combination of vectors A and B. Since O is the circumcenter, OA = OB = OC. But since C is the origin here, OC is just the distance from O to the origin, which is |O|. So OA = |O - A|, OB = |O - B|, and OC = |O|. All these must be equal.\n\nSo we have the equations:\n\n1. |O - A| = |O - B|\n2. |O - A| = |O|\n\nLet me write these out. Starting with the first one:\n\n|O - A| = |O - B|\n\nSquaring both sides:\n\n|O - A|\u00b2 = |O - B|\u00b2\n\nExpanding both sides:\n\n(O - A) \u00b7 (O - A) = (O - B) \u00b7 (O - B)\n\nWhich simplifies to:\n\n|O|\u00b2 - 2 O \u00b7 A + |A|\u00b2 = |O|\u00b2 - 2 O \u00b7 B + |B|\u00b2\n\nCancel out |O|\u00b2 from both sides:\n\n-2 O \u00b7 A + |A|\u00b2 = -2 O \u00b7 B + |B|\u00b2\n\nRearranged:\n\n2 O \u00b7 (B - A) = |B|\u00b2 - |A|\u00b2\n\nSimilarly, the second equation |O - A| = |O|, squaring both sides:\n\n|O - A|\u00b2 = |O|\u00b2\n\nWhich gives:\n\n|O|\u00b2 - 2 O \u00b7 A + |A|\u00b2 = |O|\u00b2\n\nCancelling |O|\u00b2:\n\n-2 O \u00b7 A + |A|\u00b2 = 0 => 2 O \u00b7 A = |A|\u00b2\n\nSo now I have two equations:\n\n1. 2 O \u00b7 (B - A) = |B|\u00b2 - |A|\u00b2\n2. 2 O \u00b7 A = |A|\u00b2\n\nGiven that O is expressed in terms of A and B via the vector equation:\n\nO = t A + (1/2 - 3t/4) B\n\nLet me substitute this expression for O into the two equations above.\n\nFirst, substitute O into equation 2:\n\n2 (t A + (1/2 - 3t/4) B) \u00b7 A = |A|\u00b2\n\nCompute the dot product:\n\n2 [ t |A|\u00b2 + (1/2 - 3t/4)(B \u00b7 A) ] = |A|\u00b2\n\nSimilarly, substitute O into equation 1:\n\n2 (t A + (1/2 - 3t/4) B) \u00b7 (B - A) = |B|\u00b2 - |A|\u00b2\n\nLet me compute the left-hand side:\n\n2 [ t A \u00b7 (B - A) + (1/2 - 3t/4) B \u00b7 (B - A) ]\n\n= 2 [ t (A \u00b7 B - |A|\u00b2) + (1/2 - 3t/4)(|B|\u00b2 - A \u00b7 B) ]\n\nSo now we have two equations with variables |A|\u00b2, |B|\u00b2, A \u00b7 B, and the parameter t. But this seems complicated. Maybe there's a way to relate these equations.\n\nBut wait, we also know that |AB| = 3. Since AB is the vector from A to B, its length is |B - A| = 3. So:\n\n|B - A|\u00b2 = 9\n\nWhich expands to:\n\n|B|\u00b2 - 2 A \u00b7 B + |A|\u00b2 = 9\n\nSo that's another equation connecting |A|\u00b2, |B|\u00b2, and A \u00b7 B.\n\nSo now, we have three equations:\n\n1. From equation 2: 2 [ t |A|\u00b2 + (1/2 - 3t/4)(B \u00b7 A) ] = |A|\u00b2\n2. From equation 1: 2 [ t (A \u00b7 B - |A|\u00b2) + (1/2 - 3t/4)(|B|\u00b2 - A \u00b7 B) ] = |B|\u00b2 - |A|\u00b2\n3. From |AB| = 3: |B|\u00b2 - 2 A \u00b7 B + |A|\u00b2 = 9\n\nThis seems like a system of three equations with variables |A|\u00b2, |B|\u00b2, A \u00b7 B, and t.\n\nHmm, this might be a bit too involved. Maybe I can introduce variables to simplify. Let me denote:\n\nLet\u2019s let x = |A|\u00b2, y = |B|\u00b2, z = A \u00b7 B.\n\nThen, equation 3 becomes:\n\ny - 2 z + x = 9 --> x + y - 2 z = 9.\n\nEquation 2 (from the second equation above):\n\n2 [ t (z - x) + (1/2 - 3t/4)(y - z) ] = y - x\n\nExpanding that:\n\n2 t (z - x) + 2*(1/2 - 3t/4)(y - z) = y - x\n\nSimplify:\n\n2t(z - x) + (1 - 3t/2)(y - z) = y - x\n\nSimilarly, equation 1 (from the first equation):\n\n2 [ t x + (1/2 - 3t/4) z ] = x\n\nSimplify:\n\n2 t x + 2*(1/2 - 3t/4) z = x\n\nWhich is:\n\n2 t x + (1 - 3t/2) z = x\n\nSo now, the three equations in terms of x, y, z, t are:\n\n1. 2 t x + (1 - 3t/2) z = x --> 2 t x + (1 - 3t/2) z - x = 0 --> (2 t - 1) x + (1 - 3t/2) z = 0\n\n2. 2t(z - x) + (1 - 3t/2)(y - z) = y - x\n\n3. x + y - 2 z = 9\n\nSo equation 1: (2t - 1) x + (1 - 3t/2) z = 0\n\nEquation 2: 2t(z - x) + (1 - 3t/2)(y - z) - y + x = 0\n\nLet me expand equation 2 step by step.\n\nFirst, expand 2t(z - x):\n\n2t z - 2t x\n\nThen expand (1 - 3t/2)(y - z):\n\n(1 - 3t/2)y - (1 - 3t/2) z\n\nSo putting it all together:\n\n2t z - 2t x + (1 - 3t/2)y - (1 - 3t/2) z - y + x = 0\n\nCombine like terms:\n\nFor y terms: (1 - 3t/2)y - y = (1 - 3t/2 - 1)y = (-3t/2)y\n\nFor z terms: 2t z - (1 - 3t/2) z = [2t - 1 + 3t/2] z = [ (4t/2 - 1 + 3t/2) ] z = [ (7t/2 - 1) ] z\n\nFor x terms: -2t x + x = (-2t + 1) x\n\nSo equation 2 becomes:\n\n(-3t/2) y + (7t/2 - 1) z + (-2t + 1) x = 0\n\nSo equation 2: (-2t + 1) x + (-3t/2) y + (7t/2 - 1) z = 0\n\nNow, equations 1 and 2 are:\n\nEquation 1: (2t - 1) x + (1 - 3t/2) z = 0\n\nEquation 2: (-2t + 1) x + (-3t/2) y + (7t/2 - 1) z = 0\n\nEquation 3: x + y - 2 z = 9\n\nSo now, we have a system of three equations with variables x, y, z.\n\nLet me write this in matrix form or try to solve for variables.\n\nLet me denote equations as:\n\n1. (2t - 1) x + (0) y + (1 - 3t/2) z = 0\n\n2. (-2t + 1) x + (-3t/2) y + (7t/2 - 1) z = 0\n\n3. x + y - 2 z = 9\n\nSo perhaps we can express equations 1 and 2 to eliminate variables.\n\nFrom equation 1: solve for x in terms of z.\n\nEquation 1:\n\n(2t - 1) x = (3t/2 - 1) z\n\nTherefore,\n\nx = [(3t/2 - 1)/(2t - 1)] z\n\nSimilarly, from equation 3: x + y = 2 z + 9\n\nSo y = 2 z + 9 - x\n\nSubstitute x from equation 1 into this:\n\ny = 2 z + 9 - [(3t/2 - 1)/(2t - 1)] z\n\n= [2 - (3t/2 - 1)/(2t - 1)] z + 9\n\nLet me compute 2 - (3t/2 - 1)/(2t - 1):\n\nFirst, write 2 as 2*(2t - 1)/(2t - 1) to have the same denominator:\n\n= [2*(2t - 1) - (3t/2 - 1)] / (2t - 1)\n\nCompute numerator:\n\n2*(2t - 1) = 4t - 2\n\nSubtract (3t/2 - 1):\n\n4t - 2 - 3t/2 + 1 = (4t - 3t/2) + (-2 + 1) = (5t/2) - 1\n\nTherefore:\n\n2 - (3t/2 -1)/(2t -1) = (5t/2 -1)/(2t -1)\n\nHence, y = [(5t/2 -1)/(2t -1)] z + 9\n\nNow, substitute x and y into equation 2.\n\nEquation 2:\n\n(-2t + 1) x + (-3t/2) y + (7t/2 -1) z = 0\n\nSubstitute x = [(3t/2 -1)/(2t -1)] z\n\nand y = [(5t/2 -1)/(2t -1)] z + 9\n\nSo:\n\n(-2t +1)[(3t/2 -1)/(2t -1)] z + (-3t/2)[(5t/2 -1)/(2t -1) z + 9] + (7t/2 -1) z = 0\n\nLet me compute each term step by step.\n\nFirst term: (-2t +1)[(3t/2 -1)/(2t -1)] z\n\nNote that (-2t +1) = - (2t -1), so:\n\n- (2t -1) * [ (3t/2 -1)/(2t -1) ] z = - (3t/2 -1) z\n\nSecond term: (-3t/2)[(5t/2 -1)/(2t -1) z + 9]\n\n= (-3t/2)*(5t/2 -1)/(2t -1) z - (27t/2)\n\nThird term: (7t/2 -1) z\n\nPutting all together:\n\n- (3t/2 -1) z - (3t/2)*(5t/2 -1)/(2t -1) z - (27t/2) + (7t/2 -1) z = 0\n\nLet me combine the terms with z:\n\n[ - (3t/2 -1) - (3t/2)*(5t/2 -1)/(2t -1) + (7t/2 -1) ] z - (27t/2) = 0\n\nLet me compute the coefficient of z step by step.\n\nFirst term: - (3t/2 -1) = -3t/2 +1\n\nSecond term: - (3t/2)*(5t/2 -1)/(2t -1)\n\nThird term: + (7t/2 -1)\n\nSo combine all:\n\n[ (-3t/2 +1) + ( - (3t/2)*(5t/2 -1)/(2t -1) ) + (7t/2 -1) ] z - (27t/2) = 0\n\nSimplify the constants and t terms:\n\n(-3t/2 +1 +7t/2 -1) = (4t/2) = 2t\n\nSo:\n\n[ 2t - (3t/2)*(5t/2 -1)/(2t -1) ] z - (27t/2) = 0\n\nTherefore:\n\n[ 2t - (3t/2)*(5t/2 -1)/(2t -1) ] z = (27t/2)\n\nLet me compute the coefficient of z:\n\nLet me write 2t as [2t*(2t -1)/(2t -1)] to have a common denominator:\n\n= [ 2t*(2t -1) - (3t/2)*(5t/2 -1) ] / (2t -1)\n\nCompute numerator:\n\nFirst term: 2t*(2t -1) = 4t\u00b2 - 2t\n\nSecond term: - (3t/2)*(5t/2 -1) = - (15t\u00b2/4 - 3t/2 )\n\nSo numerator:\n\n4t\u00b2 - 2t -15t\u00b2/4 + 3t/2\n\nConvert all terms to quarters:\n\n= 16t\u00b2/4 - 8t/4 -15t\u00b2/4 + 6t/4\n\n= (16t\u00b2 -15t\u00b2)/4 + (-8t +6t)/4\n\n= t\u00b2/4 - 2t/4\n\n= (t\u00b2 - 2t)/4\n\nTherefore, coefficient of z:\n\n[ (t\u00b2 - 2t)/4 ] / (2t -1) = (t(t - 2))/ [4(2t -1) ]\n\nSo the equation becomes:\n\n[ t(t - 2) / (4(2t -1)) ] z = 27t/2\n\nAssuming t \u2260 0 (since t \u2208 \u211d \\ {0}), we can divide both sides by t:\n\n(t - 2)/(4(2t -1)) z = 27/