ptyset$.\n\nNext, analyze each case:\n1. **Case (a): $\\{1\\} \\cup T$** \n    - Here, the smallest element is $1$, which is odd. \n    - Hence, any subset formed in this manner has an odd smallest element.\n2. **Case (b): $\\{2\\} \\cup T$**\n    - The smallest element here is $2$, which is even.\n3. **Case (c): $\\{1, 2\\} \\cup T$**\n    - Here, the smallest element is still $1$, which is odd.\n    - Thus, any subset formed in this manner also has an odd smallest element.\n4. **Case (d): $T \\subseteq \\{3, 4, \\ldots, 2n+2\\}$ where $T \\neq \\emptyset$**\n    - By our inductive hypothesis, exactly $\\frac{2}{3}$ of these subsets have an odd smallest element. We are essentially considering the problem on the set $\\{3, 4, \\ldots, 2n+2\\}$, which is of size $2n$.\n\nEach of the three forms (a), (b), and (c) occur equally often:\n- Forms (a) and (c) have an odd smallest element, while form (b) does not.\n- Therefore, in these cases, $\\frac{2}{3}$ subsets have an odd smallest element.\n\nBy examining subsets across all categories equally and considering the cases for $T$, the overall proportion still holds.\n\nThus, by induction, the proportion of non-empty subsets of $\\{1, 2, \\ldots, 2n\\}$ with an odd smallest element is $\\boxed{\\frac{2}{3}}$.", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Calculate: \\( 4\\left(\\sin ^{3} \\frac{49 \\pi}{48} \\cos \\frac{49 \\pi}{16} + \\cos ^{3} \\frac{49 \\pi}{48} \\sin \\frac{49 \\pi}{16}\\right) \\cos \\frac{49 \\pi}{12} \\).", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, let me try to work through this problem step by step. The expression given is a bit complicated with multiple trigonometric functions and angles. Let me write it down again to make sure I have it right:\n\n\\( 4\\left(\\sin ^{3} \\frac{49 \\pi}{48} \\cos \\frac{49 \\pi}{16} + \\cos ^{3} \\frac{49 \\pi}{48} \\sin \\frac{49 \\pi}{16}\\right) \\cos \\frac{49 \\pi}{12} \\)\n\nHmm. So, the structure is 4 times a combin