nL = \\int_{a}^{b} \\sqrt{1 + \\left( f'(x) \\right)^2} \\, dx\n\\]\n\n**Step 1: Find the derivative of \\( f(x) \\)**\n\\[\nf'(x) = \\frac{d}{dx} \\ln(x^2 - 1) = \\frac{2x}{x^2 - 1}\n\\]\n\n**Step 2: Compute the integrand**\n\\[\n\\left( f'(x) \\right)^2 = \\left( \\frac{2x}{x^2 - 1} \\right)^2 = \\frac{4x^2}{(x^2 - 1)^2}\n\\]\n\\[\n1 + \\left( f'(x) \\right)^2 = 1 + \\frac{4x^2}{(x^2 - 1)^2} = \\frac{(x^2 - 1)^2 + 4x^2}{(x^2 - 1)^2} = \\frac{(x^2 + 1)^2}{(x^2 - 1)^2}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\sqrt{1 + \\left( f'(x) \\right)^2} = \\frac{x^2 + 1}{x^2 - 1}\n\\]\n\n**Step 3: Simplify the integrand**\n\\[\n\\frac{x^2 + 1}{x^2 - 1} = 1 + \\frac{2}{x^2 - 1}\n\\]\n\n**Step 4: Integrate from 2 to 3**\n\\[\nL = \\int_{2}^{3} \\left( 1 + \\frac{2}{x^2 - 1} \\right) \\, dx\n\\]\n\\[\n\\int \\frac{2}{x^2 - 1} \\, dx = 2 \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\ln \\left| \\frac{x - 1}{x + 1} \\right| + C = \\ln \\left| \\frac{x - 1}{x + 1} \\right| + C\n\\]\n\\[\nL = \\left[ x + \\ln \\left( \\frac{x - 1}{x + 1} \\right) \\right]_{2}^{3}\n\\]\n\n**Step 5: Evaluate the definite integral**\n\\[\nL = \\left[ 3 + \\ln \\left( \\frac{2}{4} \\right) \\right] - \\left[ 2 + \\ln \\left( \\frac{1}{3} \\right) \\right]\n\\]\n\\[\n= 3 + \\ln \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right) - 2 - \\ln \\left( \\frac{1}{3} \\right)\n\\]\n\\[\n= 1 + \\ln \\left( \\frac{3}{2} \\right)\n\\]\n\nThus, the arc length of the curve is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{1 + \\ln\\left(\\dfrac{3}{2}\\right)}\n\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "\nTo find the arc length of the curve given by the equation \\( y = \\ln(x^2 - 1) \\) for the interval \\( 2 \\leq x \\leq 3 \\), we use the arc length formula for a function \\( y = f(x) \\) defined as:\n\n\\[\nL = \\int_{a}^{b} \\sqrt{1 + \\left(f'(x)\\right)^2} \\, dx\n\\]\n\n1. **Find the derivative \\( f'(x) \\):**\n\nGiven \\( f(x) = \\ln(x^2 - 1) \\):\n\n\\[\nf'(x) = \\frac{d}{dx} \\left[ \\ln(x^2 - 1) \\right]\n\\]\n\nUsing the chain rule,\n\n\\[\nf'(x) = \\frac{1}{x^2 - 1} \\cdot \\frac{d}{dx}(x^2 - 1) = \\frac{1}{x^2 - 1} \\cdot 2x = \\frac{2x}{x^2 - 1}\n\\]\n\n2. **Set up the arc length integral:**\n\nSubstitute \\( f'(x) \\) into the arc length formula:\n\n\\[\nL = \\int_{2}^{3} \\sqrt{1 + \\left(\\frac{2x}{x^2 - 1}\\right)^2} \\, dx\n\\]\n\n3. **Simplify the integrand:**\n\nFirst, compute \\( \\left(\\frac{2x}{x^2 - 1}\\right)^2 \\):\n\n\\[\n\\left(\\frac{2x}{x^2 - 1}\\right)^2 = \\frac{4x^2}{(x^2 - 1)^2}\n\\]\n\nThus,\n\n\\[\n1 + \\left(\\frac{2x}{x^2 - 1}\\right)^2 = 1 + \\frac{4x^2}{(x^2 - 1)^2}\n\\]\n\nCombine these into a single fraction:\n\n\\[\n1 + \\frac{4x^2}{(x^2 - 1)^2} = \\frac{(x^2 - 1)^2 + 4x^2}{(x^2 - 1)^2} = \\frac{x^4 - 2x^2 + 1 + 4x^2}{(x^2 - 1)^2} = \\frac{x^4 + 2x^2 + 1}{(x^2 - 1)^2}\n\\]\n\nNotice that \\( x^4 + 2x^2 + 1 = (x^2 + 1)^2 \\), hence:\n\n\\[\n\\sqrt{1 + \\left(\\frac{2x}{x^2 - 1}\\right)^2} = \\sqrt{\\frac{(x^2 + 1)^2}{(x^2 - 1)^2}} = \\frac{x^2 + 1}{x^2 - 1}\n\\]\n\n4. **Evaluate the integral:**\n\n\\[\nL = \\int_{2}^{3} \\frac{x^2 + 1}{x^2 - 1} \\, dx\n\\]\n\nSplit the integrand:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{x^2 + 1}{x^2 - 1} = \\frac{x^2 - 1 + 2}{x^2 - 1} = \\frac{x^2 - 1}{x^2 - 1} + \\frac{2}{x^2 - 1} = 1 + \\frac{2}{x^2 - 1}\n\\]\n\nNow, integrate term-by-term:\n\n\\[\nL = \\int_{2}^{3} 1 \\, dx + 2 \\int_{2}^{3} \\frac{1}{x^2 - 1} \\, dx\n\\]\n\nThe first integral is straightforward:\n\n\\[\n\\int_{2}^{3} 1 \\, dx = x \\Big|_{2}^{3} = 3 - 2 = 1\n\\]\n\nFor the second integral, note that:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{x^2 - 1} = \\frac{1}{(x - 1)(x + 1)}\n\\]\n\nDecompose into partial fractions:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{(x - 1)(x + 1)} = \\frac{A}{x - 1} + \\frac{B}{x + 1}\n\\]\n\nSolving for \\( A \\) and \\( B \\):\n\n\\[\n1 = A(x + 1) + B(x - 1)\n\\]\nSetting \\( x = 1 \\):\n\n\\[\n1 = A(1 + 1) \\implies A = \\frac{1}{2}\n\\]\n\nSetting \\( x = -1 \\):\n\n\\[\n1 = B(-1 - 1) \\implies B = -\\frac{1}{2}\n\\]\n\nThus,\n\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{x^2 - 1} = \\frac{1/2}{x - 1} - \\frac{1/2}{x + 1}\n\\]\n\nIntegrate these terms separately:\n\n\\[\n2 \\int_{2}^{3} \\frac{1}{x^2 - 1} \\, 