c{A + C - B}{2}\\right)\n\\]\n\nUsing the identity \\(\\cos \\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2} - x\\right) = \\sin x\\):\n\n\\[\n= \\sin C + \\sin B\n\\]\n\n5. By the same method, we can similarly find:\n\n\\[\nB B_{1} \\cdot \\cos \\frac{B}{2} = \\sin A + \\sin C\n\\]\n\\[\nC C_{1} \\cdot \\cos \\frac{C}{2} = \\sin A + \\sin B\n\\]\n\n6. Summing these results together:\n\n\\[\nA A_{1} \\cdot \\cos \\frac{A}{2} + B B_{1} \\cdot \\cos \\frac{B}{2} + C C_{1} \\cdot \\cos \\frac{C}{2} = (\\sin B + \\sin C) + (\\sin A + \\sin C) + (\\sin A + \\sin B)\n\\]\n\n7. Simplifying further:\n\n\\[\n= 2 (\\sin A + \\sin B + \\sin C)\n\\]\n\n8. The given expression therefore is:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{2 (\\sin A + \\sin B + \\sin C)}{\\sin A + \\sin B + \\sin C} = 2\n\\]\n\nConclusion, the value is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Let \\( P \\) be a 40-sided convex polygon. Find the number of triangles \\( S \\) formed by the vertices of \\( P \\) such that any two vertices of \\( S \\) are separated by at least two other vertices of \\( P \\).", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to figure out how many triangles can be formed from a 40-sided convex polygon where each pair of vertices in the triangle are separated by at least two other vertices. Hmm, let me break this down step by step. \n\nFirst, let me visualize a convex polygon with 40 sides. Each vertex is connected to its adjacent vertices with edges, and the polygon is convex, so all the interior angles are less than 180 degrees. Now, the problem is about selecting three vertices such that any two selected vertices are not adjacent and not just separated by one vertex, but at least two vertices in between. So, between any two vertices of the triangle, there must be at least two other vertices of the polygon. That means the minimum number of edges between any two selected vertices on the polygon should be three. Because if two vertices are separated 