riangle to the opposite sides, the following holds:\n\n[sin(\u2220PAB)/sin(\u2220PAC)] * [sin(\u2220PBC)/sin(\u2220PBA)] * [sin(\u2220PCA)/sin(\u2220PCB)] = 1\n\nWait, let me recall the exact statement. Trigonometric Ceva's theorem states that if three cevians AD, BE, CF concur at a point P, then:\n\n[sin(\u2220BAD)/sin(\u2220CAD)] * [sin(\u2220CBE)/sin(\u2220ABE)] * [sin(\u2220ACF)/sin(\u2220BCF)] = 1\n\nIn our case, the cevians are AP, BP, CP. So, applying trigonometric Ceva's theorem here, we can write:\n\n[sin(\u2220PAB)/sin(\u2220PAC)] * [sin(\u2220PBC)/sin(\u2220PBA)] * [sin(\u2220PCA)/sin(\u2220PCB)] = 1\n\nPlugging in the known angles:\n\n\u2220PAB = 10\u00b0, \u2220PAC = 40\u00b0, \u2220PBA = 20\u00b0, \u2220PCA = 30\u00b0, and \u2220PBC and \u2220PCB are variables we denoted earlier.\n\nSo:\n\n[sin(10\u00b0)/sin(40\u00b0)] * [sin(\u2220PBC)/sin(20\u00b0)] * [sin(30\u00b0)/sin(\u2220PCB)] = 1\n\nWe also know from earlier that \u2220PBC + \u2220PCB = 80\u00b0, so \u2220PCB = 80\u00b0 - \u2220PBC. Let me substitute that in:\n\n[sin(10\u00b0)/sin(40\u00b0)] * [sin(\u2220PBC)/sin(20\u00b0)] * [sin(30\u00b0)/sin(80\u00b0 - \u2220PBC)] = 1\n\nLet me denote x = \u2220PBC for simplicity. Then the equation becomes:\n\n[sin(10\u00b0)/sin(40\u00b0)] * [sin(x)/sin(20\u00b0)] * [sin(30\u00b0)/sin(80\u00b0 - x)] = 1\n\nWe need to solve for x. Let's write this equation as:\n\n[sin(10\u00b0) * sin(x) * sin(30\u00b0)] / [sin(40\u00b0) * sin(20\u00b0) * sin(80\u00b0 - x)] = 1\n\nMultiply both sides by the denominator:\n\nsin(10\u00b0) * sin(x) * sin(30\u00b0) = sin(40\u00b0) * sin(20\u00b0) * sin(80\u00b0 - x)\n\nHmm, this looks a bit complicated, but maybe we can simplify step by step. Let's compute sin(30\u00b0) which is 0.5. So:\n\nsin(10\u00b0) * sin(x) * 0.5 = sin(40\u00b0) * sin(20\u00b0) * sin(80\u00b0 - x)\n\nLet me compute the left-hand side (LHS) and right-hand side (RHS) separately. Let's plug in some known values.\n\nFirst, compute LHS: 0.5 * sin(10\u00b0) * sin(x)\n\nRHS: sin(40\u00b0) * sin(20\u00b0) * sin(80\u00b0 - x)\n\nAlternatively, maybe we can use trigonometric identities to simplify. Let me recall that sin(A)sin(B) = [cos(A-B) - cos(A+B)]/2. Maybe this can be helpful.\n\nAlternatively, since we have sin(80\u00b0 - x), perhaps there's a way to relate angles. Let me see:\n\nNote that 80\u00b0 - x = 80\u00b0 - \u2220PBC. But \u2220PBC + \u2220PCB = 80\u00b0, so 80\u00b0 - x = \u2220PCB. Wait, that's exactly how we defined it earlier. So sin(80\u00b0 - x) = sin(\u2220PCB). Hmm, but maybe this isn't directly helpful.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can use substitution or look for an angle x that satisfies the equation. Let's try plugging in x = 30\u00b0. Let's check if that works.\n\nIf x = 30\u00b0, then:\n\nLHS = 0.5 * sin(10\u00b0) * sin(30\u00b0) = 0.5 * sin(10\u00b0) * 0.5 = 0.25 * sin(10\u00b0)\n\nRHS = sin(40\u00b0) * sin(20\u00b0) * sin(50\u00b0) (since 80\u00b0 - 30\u00b0 = 50\u00b0)\n\nCompute approximate values:\n\nsin(10\u00b0) \u2248 0.1736\nsin(20\u00b0) \u2248 0.3420\nsin(40\u00b0) \u2248 0.6428\nsin(50\u00b0) \u2248 0.7660\n\nSo LHS \u2248 0.25 * 0.1736 \u2248 0.0434\n\nRHS \u2248 0.6428 * 0.3420 * 0.7660 \u2248 0.6428 * 0.262 \u2248 0.168\n\nNot equal, so x=30\u00b0 is not a solution.\n\nLet's try x=20\u00b0:\n\nLHS = 0.5 * sin(10\u00b0) * sin(20\u00b0) \u2248 0.5 * 0.1736 * 0.3420 \u2248 0.5 * 0.0593 \u2248 0.0296\n\nRHS = sin(40\u00b0)*sin(20\u00b0)*sin(60\u00b0) \u2248 0.6428 * 0.3420 * 0.8660 \u2248 0.6428 * 0.296 \u2248 0.190\n\nStill not equal. Hmm.\n\nWait, maybe x= 10\u00b0?\n\nLHS = 0.5 * sin(10\u00b0)*sin(10\u00b0) \u2248 0.5 * 0.1736^2 \u2248 0.5 * 0.0301 \u2248 0.01505\n\nRHS = sin(40\u00b0)*sin(20\u00b0)*sin(70\u00b0) \u2248 0.6428 * 0.3420 * 0.9397 \u2248 0.6428 * 0.321 \u2248 0.206\n\nStill not matching. Maybe x=40\u00b0?\n\nThen, 80\u00b0 - x=40\u00b0, so:\n\nLHS=0.5*sin(10\u00b0)*sin(40\u00b0)\u22480.5*0.1736*0.6428\u22480.5*0.1115\u22480.0557\n\nRHS=sin(40\u00b0)*sin(20\u00b0)*sin(40\u0