merator is:\n   $$\n   N'(x) = 5^x \\cos 2x [(\\ln 5)^2 + 4].\n   $$\n\n7. Divide by the denominator \\( 4 + (\\ln 5)^2 \\):\n   $$\n   y' = \\frac{5^x \\cos 2x [(\\ln 5)^2 + 4]}{4 + (\\ln 5)^2} = 5^x \\cos 2x.\n   $$\n\nThe derivative of the function is \n\n$$\n\\boxed{5^{x} \\cos 2x}.\n```", "ground_truth_solution": "\nWe need to find the derivative of the function \n\n\\[\ny = \\frac{5^{x} (2 \\sin 2x + \\cos 2x \\cdot \\ln 5)}{4 + (\\ln 5)^2}\n\\]\n\nLet\u2019s denote:\n\n1. \\(u = 5^{x}\\)\n2. \\(v = 2 \\sin 2x + \\cos 2x \\cdot \\ln 5\\)\n3. \\(w = 4 + (\\ln 5)^2\\)\n\nThe function can be rewritten as \\( y = \\frac{u \\cdot v}{w} \\). Since \\(w\\) is a constant, the differentiation can be simplified. \n\nBy applying the quotient rule for derivatives, $\\left(\\frac{u \\cdot v}{w}\\right)' = \\frac{u' \\cdot v + u \\cdot v'}{w}$, we get the following steps.\n\n### Step 1: Differentiate \\(u\\):\n\n\\[\nu = 5^x\n\\]\n\nUsing the chain rule:\n\n\\[\nu' = 5^x \\cdot \\ln 5\n\\]\n\n### Step 2: Differentiate \\(v\\):\n\n\\[\nv = 2 \\sin 2x + \\cos 2x \\cdot \\ln 5\n\\]\n\nUsing the chain rule and basic differentiation rules:\n\n\\[\nv' = \\frac{d}{dx}(2 \\sin 2x) + \\frac{d}{dx} (\\cos 2x \\cdot \\ln 5)\n\\]\n\nFirst term:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{d}{dx}(2 \\sin 2x) = 2 \\cdot 2 \\cos 2x = 4 \\cos 2x\n\\]\n\nSecond term:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{d}{dx}(\\cos 2x \\cdot \\ln 5) = \\ln 5 \\cdot \\frac{d}{dx}(\\cos 2x) \n\\]\n\nSince \\( \\frac{d}{dx}(\\cos 2x) = -2 \\sin 2x \\):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{d}{dx}(\\cos 2x \\cdot \\ln 5) = \\ln 5 \\cdot (-2 \\sin 2x) = -2 \\sin 2x \\cdot \\ln 5\n\\]\n\nCombining these:\n\n\\[\nv' = 4 \\cos 2x - 2 \\sin 2x \\cdot \\ln 5\n\\]\n\n### Step 3: Combine the differentiated parts into the quotient rule formula:\n\n\\[\ny' = \\frac{u' \\cdot v + u \\cdot v'}{w}\n\\]\n\nSubstitute the expressions for \\(u\\), \\(u'\\), \\(v\\), and \\(v'\\):\n\n\\[\ny' = \\frac{5^x \\cdot \\ln 5 \\cdot (2 \\sin 2x + \\cos 2x \\cdot \\ln 5) + 5^x \\cdot (4 \\cos 2x - 2 \\sin 2x 