\quad \\text{(similar reasoning as above)}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Simplify and maximize \\((y-1)(2-x) + 2\\):**\n   The product \\((y-1)(2-x)\\) will achieve its maximum when \\(y-1\\) and \\(2-x\\) are at their extents:\n   \\[\n   -22 \\leq y-1 \\leq 20 \\quad \\text{which translates to} \\quad -22 \\text{ and } 20 \n   \\]\n   \\[\n   -19 \\leq 2-x \\leq 23 \\quad \\text{which translates to} \\quad -19 \\text{ and } 23\n   \\]\n\n5. **Finding maximum values:**\n   Consider:\n   \\[\n   (y - 1)(2 - x) = 20 \\cdot 23\n   \\]\n\n6. **Calculate:**\n   \\[\n   20 \\cdot 23 + 2 = 460 + 2 = 462\n   \\]\n\n7. **Conclusion**:\n   Therefore, the maximum value is achieved under the conditions described and simplifies to:\n   \\[\n   a = c = -10.5, b = d = 10.5\n   \\]\n\nThus, the maximum value is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{462}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "In trapezoid \\(ABCD\\), the side \\(CD\\) is perpendicular to the bases, and \\(O\\) is the intersection point of the diagonals. A point \\(S\\) is taken on the circumcircle of triangle \\(OCD\\), diametrically opposite to point \\(O\\). Prove that \\(\\angle BSC = \\angle ASD\\).", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, let me try to figure out how to prove that angle BSC is equal to angle ASD in this trapezoid problem. Hmm, so first, let me visualize the trapezoid ABCD. It's given that CD is perpendicular to the bases, so that means CD is the height of the trapezoid. Also, O is the intersection point of the diagonals. Then there's a point S on the circumcircle of triangle OCD, diametrically opposite to O. I need to show that angle BSC equals angle ASD.\n\nLet me start by drawing a diagram to get a better sense. Trapezoid ABCD with bases AB and CD, since CD is perpendicular to the bases, so sides AD and BC are the non-parallel sides. Diagonals AC and BD intersect at point O. Then the circumcircle of triangle OCD... so points O, C, D are on a circle, and S is diametrically