tten as:\n\nx = 4 - (3/2)t\n\ny = \u221a3/3 + (13\u221a3/6 )t\n\nwhere t is a parameter. When t=0, we are at O, and when t=1, we reach A.\n\nBut since \u0393 is inside the circumcircle, the center O_\u0393 is somewhere between O and A, so t is between 0 and 1.\n\nLet the center of \u0393 be O_\u0393 = (4 - (3/2)t, \u221a3/3 + (13\u221a3/6 )t )\n\nThe radius r of \u0393 is R - distance between O and O_\u0393. Since O_\u0393 is on the line towards A, the distance between O and O_\u0393 is t * |OA|. Wait, but OA is the distance from O to A. Let's compute that.\n\nDistance OA: sqrt[ (5/2 -4)^2 + ( (5\u221a3)/2 - \u221a3/3 )^2 ]\n\nWe already calculated the vector components as (-3/2, 13\u221a3/6 )\n\nThus, distance OA: sqrt[ ( (-3/2)^2 + (13\u221a3/6 )^2 ) ]\n\nCalculate:\n\n(-3/2)^2 = 9/4\n\n(13\u221a3/6 )^2 = (169*3)/36 = 507/36 = 169/12\n\nSo OA distance: sqrt(9/4 + 169/12 )= sqrt(27/12 + 169/12 )= sqrt(196/12 )=sqrt(49/3)=7/\u221a3, which is equal to R. That makes sense because A is on the circumcircle.\n\nWait, so the distance from O to A is exactly R=7/\u221a3. Therefore, if O_\u0393 is a point along OA at a distance R - r from O, then the distance from O to O_\u0393 is R - r. But since the total length OA is R, the distance from O_\u0393 to A is R - (R - r ) = r.\n\nBut O_\u0393 is the center of \u0393, and \u0393 passes through A, so the radius of \u0393 is the distance from O_\u0393 to A, which is r. Therefore, indeed, r = distance from O_\u0393 to A. But also, since \u0393 is tangent to BC, the distance from O_\u0393 to BC must be equal to r.\n\nSo we have two expressions for r:\n\n1. r = distance from O_\u0393 to A = |O_\u0393A|\n\n2. r = distance from O_\u0393 to BC\n\nThus, setting these equal, we can solve for t.\n\nFirst, let's express O_\u0393 in terms of t:\n\nO_\u0393 = (4 - (3/2)t, \u221a3/3 + (13\u221a3/6 )t )\n\nDistance from O_\u0393 to A(5/2, (5\u221a3)/2 ):\n\nx-coordinate difference: (4 - (3/2)t -5/2 ) = (8/2 -5/2 - (3/2)t ) = (3/2 - (3/2)t ) = 