( dx \\, dy \\)**:\n   - For \\( y \\) ranging from \\( -1 \\) to \\( 3 \\), the horizontal slices are bounded on the left by \\( x = -\\sqrt{3 - y} \\) and on the right by \\( x = \\sqrt{3 - y} \\).\n   - The iterated integral is:\n     \\[\n     \\int_{-1}^{3} \\int_{-\\sqrt{3 - y}}^{\\sqrt{3 - y}} f(x, y) \\, dx \\, dy\n     \\]\n\n### Final Answer\nThe double integral of \\( f(x, y) \\) over the region \\( D \\) can be expressed as the following iterated integrals:\n\nIn the order \\( dy\\,dx \\):\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\int_{-2}^{2} \\int_{-1}^{3 - x^2} f(x, y) \\, dy \\, dx}\n\\]\n\nIn the order \\( dx\\,dy \\):\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\int_{-1}^{3} \\int_{-\\sqrt{3 - y}}^{\\sqrt{3 - y}} f(x, y) \\, dx \\, dy}\n\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. **Determine the Intersection Points:**\n   - Solve the system of equations \\(y = 3 - x^2\\) and \\(y = -1\\) to find the points of intersection:\n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   &\\text{From \\( y = -1 \\) we substitute into \\( y = 3 - x^2 \\)} \\\\\n   &-1 = 3 - x^2 \\\\\n   &x^2 = 4 \\\\\n   &x = \\pm 2 \\\\\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n   Therefore, the intersection points are \\( A(-2, -1) \\) and \\( B(2, -1) \\).\n\n2. **Outer and Inner Integrals (Integrating First with Respect to \\( y \\)):**\n   - Consider the region \\( D \\) bounded by \\( y = 3 - x^2 \\) and \\( y = -1 \\), and project this region onto the \\( x \\)-axis, giving the interval \\([-2, 2]\\).\n   - For \\( x \\) in \\([-2, 2]\\), \\( y \\) ranges from \\(-1\\) to \\( 3 - x^2 \\):\n\n   \\[\n   \\iint_{D} f(x, y) \\, dx \\, dy = \\int_{-2}^{2} \\left( \\int_{-1}^{3 - x^2} f(x, y) \\, dy \\right) dx\n   \\]\n\n3. **Outer and Inner Integrals (Integrating First with Respect to \\( x \\)):**\n   - Now, project the region \\( D \\) onto the \\( y \\)-axis, giving the interval \\([-1, 3]\\).\n   - For \\( y \\) in \\([-1, 3]\\), \\( x \\) ranges from \\(-\\sqrt{3 - y}\\) to \\(\\sqrt{3 - y}\\) (since solving \\( y = 3 - x^2 \\) for \\( x \\) gives \\( x = \\pm \\sqrt{3 - y}\\)):\n\n   \\[\n   \\iint_{D} f(x, y) \\, dx \\, dy = \\int_{-1}^{3} \\left( \\int_{-\\sqrt{3 - y}}^{\\sqrt{3 - y}} f(x, y) \\, dx \\right) dy\n   \\]\n\n4. **Conclusion:**\n\nThe double integral of the function \\( f(x, y) \\) over the region \\( D \\) can be expressed with two different orders of integration:\n\n   \\[\n   \\iint_{D} f(x, y) \\, dx \\, dy = \\int_{-2}^{2} \\left( \\int_{-1}^{3 - x^2} f(x, y) \\, dy \\right) dx = \\int_{-1}^{3} \\left( \\int_{-\\sqrt{3 - y}}^{\\sqrt{3 - y}} f(x, y) \\, dx \\right) dy\n   \\]\n\nThus, the solution is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\int_{-2}^{2} \\left( \\int_{-1}^{3 - x^2} f(x, y) \\, dy \\right) dx = \\int_{-1}^{3} \\left( \\int_{-\\sqrt{3 - y}}^{\\sqrt{3 - y}} f(x, y) \\, dx \\right) dy}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "What is the quotient of \\(50^{50}\\) by \\(25^{25}\\)?\n(a) \\(25^{25}\\)\n(b) \\(10^{25}\\)\n(c) \\(100^{25}\\)\n(d) \\(2^{25}\\)\n(e) \\(2 \\times 25^{25}\\)", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, let me try to figure out the quotient of \\(50^{50}\\) divided by \\(25^{25}\\). Hmm, so the problem is asking for \\( \\frac{50^{50}}{25^{25}} \\), right? And the options are given as (a) to (e). Let me start by recalling some exponent rules because this seems like it's going to involve simplifying expressions with exponents.\n\nFirst, I know that when you have a quotient of powers, you can subtract the exponents if the bases are the same. But here, the bases are different: 50 and 25. So maybe I can express them in terms of the same base. Let me see, 50 is 2 times 25, right? Because 25 times 2 is 50. So maybe I can rewrite 50 as 2*25. Let me try that.\n\nSo, \\(50^{50}\\) can be written as \\((2 \\times 25)^{50}\\). Then, using the exponent rule for a product raised to a power, which is \\((ab)^n = a^n \\times b^n\\), this becomes \\(2^{50} \\times 25^{50}\\). Okay, so now the numerator is \\(2^{50} \\times 25^{50}\\), and the denomina