) additional days.\n\n6. **Find the day of the week:**\n\n   Since January 1, 1978 was a Sunday, we start from Sunday and count 6 days forward:\n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{align*}\n   \\text{Sunday} + 1 &= \\text{Monday}\\\\\n   \\text{Monday} + 1 &= \\text{Tuesday}\\\\\n   \\text{Tuesday} + 1 &= \\text{Wednesday}\\\\\n   \\text{Wednesday} + 1 &= \\text{Thursday}\\\\\n   \\text{Thursday} + 1 &= \\text{Friday}\\\\\n   \\text{Friday} + 1 &= \\text{Saturday}\n   \\end{align*}\n   \\]\n\n   Therefore, 6 days after Sunday is Saturday.\n\n### Conclusion:\nJanuary 1, 2000, is a **Saturday**.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Saturday}}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Let \\( e, f, g \\) be straight lines passing through a point but not lying in the same plane. The projection of \\( e \\) onto the plane of \\( f \\) and \\( g \\), that of \\( f \\) onto the plane of \\( g \\) and \\( e \\), and that of \\( g \\) onto the plane of \\( e \\) and \\( f \\) are denoted by \\( e_{1}, f_{1}, g_{1} \\) respectively. From a point on \\( e \\), construct a perpendicular to \\( f_{1} \\) in the plane of \\( e \\) and \\( g \\), and another perpendicular to \\( g_{1} \\) in the plane of \\( e \\) and \\( f \\); let these lines intersect \\( g \\) at \\( G \\) and \\( f \\) at \\( F \\) respectively. Is \\( FG \\) perpendicular to \\( e_{1} \\)?", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, let's try to tackle this geometry problem. It seems a bit complicated at first glance, but maybe if I break it down step by step, it'll make more sense. So, we have three lines e, f, g all passing through a single point, but they aren't all in the same plane. That means they form a sort of 3D coordinate system, right? Like the x, y, z axes intersecting at the origin, but maybe not orthogonal? Wait, the problem doesn't say they're orthogonal, just that they pass through a point and aren't coplanar. So, they form three different lines emanating from a common p