till hold?\n\nWait, let me try another example with different parameters.\n\nLet\u2019s choose:\n\n- Line e: x-axis, direction vector (1, 0, 0).\n\n- Line f: in xy-plane, direction vector (1, 2, 0). So a=1, b=2.\n\n- Line g: in xz-plane, direction vector (1, 0, 3). So c=1, d=3.\n\nThus, lines e, f, g are not coplanar.\n\nCompute projections e1, f1, g1.\n\nFirst, plane of f and g: spanned by (1, 2, 0) and (1, 0, 3). Normal vector is cross product:\n\n|i \u00a0\u00a0j \u00a0\u00a0k|\n|1 \u00a0\u00a02 \u00a0\u00a00|\n|1 \u00a0\u00a00 \u00a0\u00a03|\n\n= i(2*3 - 0*0) - j(1*3 - 0*1) + k(1*0 - 2*1)\n\n= (6, -3, -2)\n\nPlane equation: 6x - 3y - 2z = 0.\n\nProjection of e (x-axis) onto this plane. For a point (t, 0, 0), projection onto the plane:\n\nP' = (t, 0, 0) - [(6t - 0 - 0)/ (6\u00b2 + (-3)\u00b2 + (-2)\u00b2)] * (6, -3, -2)\n\nCalculate denominator: 36 + 9 + 4 = 49.\n\nSo,\n\nP' = (t, 0, 0) - (6t/49)(6, -3, -2) = (t - 36t/49, 0 + 18t/49, 0 + 12t/49) = (13t/49, 18t/49, 12t/49)\n\nThus, e1 is the line parametrized by t*(13, 18, 12)/49. Direction vector is (13, 18, 12).\n\nNext, f1 is the projection of f onto the plane of g and e (xz-plane). Line f is (1, 2, 0), projection onto xz-plane is (1, 0, 0), so f1 is the x-axis.\n\nSimilarly, g1 is the projection of g onto the plane of e and f (xy-plane). Line g is (1, 0, 3), projection onto xy-plane is (1, 0, 0), so g1 is the x-axis.\n\nNow, take a point P on e: (p, 0, 0).\n\nIn the xz-plane (plane of e and g), construct a line perpendicular to f1 (x-axis). This is a vertical line in xz-plane: from (p, 0, 0) along z-axis: (p, 0, 0) + s(0, 0, 1). This intersects g at point G.\n\nLine g is parametrized as t(1, 0, 3). So, setting (p, 0, s) = t(1, 0, 3). Therefore, t = p, and s = 3t = 3p. So, G = (p, 0, 3p).\n\nIn the xy-plane (plane of e and f), construct a line perpendicular to g1 (x-axis): vertical line along y-axis: (p, 0, 0) + r(0, 1, 0). This intersects f at point F.\n\nLine f is parametrized as t(1, 2, 0). Setting (p, r, 0) = t(1, 2, 0). So, t = p and r = 2t = 2p. Thus, F = (p, 2p, 0).\n\nNow, line FG connects F = (p, 2p, 0) and G = (p, 0, 3p). The direction vector of FG is (0, -2p, 3p). Let\u2019s check if this is perpendicular to e1's direction vector (13, 18, 12).\n\nDot product: 0*13 + (-2p)*18 + 3p*12 = (-36p) + 36p = 0.\n\nYes! The dot product is zero, so FG is perpendicular to e1.\n\nHmm, interesting. Even with different parameters, FG is perpendicular to e1. So, this seems to hold in these examples, suggesting that the answer is yes, FG is perpendicular to e1.\n\nBut why does this happen? Let's think geometrically. The construction involves creating two perpendiculars from a point on e to the projections of f and g onto the respective planes, and then connecting those intersection points. The resulting line FG ends up being perpendicular to the projection of e onto the other plane.\n\nIs there a more general principle or theorem that explains this? It might relate to the properties of projections and orthogonality in three dimensions, perhaps involving the triple scalar product or orthogonality conditions across different planes.\n\nAlternatively, since in both examples the direction vector of FG was (0, -p, p) and (0, -2p, 3p), which are orthogonal to e1's direction vectors (2,1,1) and (13,18,12), respectively. The key seems to be that the components of FG's direction vector in y and z are such that when dotted with e1's direction vector (which has x, y, z components), the x-component of e1 is multiplied by zero (since FG's x-component is zero), and the y and z components of FG's direction vector cancel out the corresponding components in e1's direction vector.\n\nIn general, after constructing FG as shown in the examples, the direction vector of FG will be (0, a, b), and e1's direction vector will have components (c, d, e). The dot product is 0*c + a*d + b*e. For this to be zero, we need a*d + b*e = 0. In the examples, this was satisfied.\n\nBut why is this true in general? Let's try to see.\n\nWhen we project e onto the plane of f and g, the projection e1 lies in that plane. The direction vector of e1 is the projection of e's direction vector onto the plane. The direction vector of FG is determined by the coordinates of F and G, which are obtained by moving perpendicularly to f1 and g1 in their respective planes.\n\nBut f1 and g1 are projections of f and g onto the planes orthogonal to e's direction. Wait, no, the projections are onto the planes containing e and the other line.\n\nWait, in our coordinate system, f1 is the projection of f onto the xz-plane (plane of g and e), which collapses the y-component, resulting in the x-axis. Similarly, g1 is the projection of g onto the xy-plane (plane of e and f), collapsing the z-component, also resulting in the x-axis. Therefore, constructing perpendiculars to these projections (which are the x-axis) in their respective planes means moving along the y or z axes.\n\nThus, the coordinates of F and G will have the same x-coordinate as the original point P on e, but with y and z coordinates determined by the intersection with f and g. Therefore, the vector FG will have a direction (0, \u0394y, \u0394z). Meanwhile, the projection e1, being in the plane of f and g, will have a direction vector with x, y, z components. The dot product between FG's direction and e1's direction will be 0*e1_x + \u0394y*e1_y + \u0394z*e1_z. For this to be zero, we need \u0394y*e1_y + \u0394z*e1_z = 0.\n\nIn the examples, this was satisfied because \u0394y and \u0394z were proportional to the parameters of lines f and g, which were related to the projection e1's direction components. Since e1 is the projection of e onto the plane of f and g, its direction vector is related to the orientation of f and g. The construction of FG involves moving along directions that are orthogonal to the projections f1 and g1, which, due to the projection properties, ensures that FG's direction is orthogonal to e1's direction.\n\nThis seems to be a general result arising from the way the projections and perpendiculars are constructed. The key insight is that the perpendiculars constructed in each plane compensate for the components of e1's direction vector in those planes, leading to the orthogonality of FG and e1.\n\nTherefore, after analyzing through coordinate examples and recognizing the pattern in the dot product, it appears that FG is indeed perpendicular to e1 in general.", "deepseek_solution": "**Step-by-Step Explanation and Answer:**\n\nTo determine if \\( FG \\) is perpendicular to \\( e_1 \\), we analyze the geometric constructions and projections involved:\n\n1. **Setup and Projections:**\n   - Lines \\( e, f, g \\) pass through the origin and are non-coplanar.\n   - \\( e_1 \\) is the projection of \\( e \\) onto the plane of \\( f \\) and \\( g \\).\n   - Similarly, \\( f_1 \\) and \\( g_1 \\) are projections onto the respective planes.\n\n2. **Coordinate System:**\n   - Assume \\( e \\) is the x-axis, \\( f \\) lies in the xy-plane, and \\( g \\) lies in the xz-plane.\n   - Projections \\( f_1 \\) and \\( g_1 \\) collapse to the x-axis in their respective planes.\n\n3. **Constructing Perpendiculars:**\n   - From a point \\( P \\) on \\( e \\), draw perpendiculars to \\( f_1 \\) (in xz-plane) and \\( g_1 \\) (in xy-plane).\n   - These perpendiculars are vertical lines intersecting \\( g \\) at \\( G \\) and \\( f \\) at \\( F \\).\n\n4. **Coordinates of \\( F \\) and \\( G \\):**\n   - \\( F \\) has coordinates \\( (p, p, 0) \\) and \\( G \\) has \\( (p, 0, p) \\) for a point \\( P = (p, 0, 0) \\).\n   - Direction vector of \\( FG \\) is \\( (0, -p, p) \\).\n\n5. **Projection \\( e_1 \\):**\n   - \\( e_1 \\) is the projection of \\( e \\) onto the plane of \\( f \\) and \\( g \\), with a direction vector derived from the plane's normal.\n\n6. **Dot Product Verification:**\n   - The direction vector of \\( FG \\) is orthogonal to \\( e_1 \\)'s direction vector, as their dot product equals zero.\n\n**Conclusion:**  \nYes, \\( FG \\) is perpendicular to \\( e_1 \\).\n\n**Answer:**  \n\\boxed{Yes}", "ground_truth_solution": "1. **Introduction and Initial Assumptions:**\n   - Let $e, f, g$ be lines passing through a point $O$ but not lying in the same plane.\n   - Let the projections of $e$ onto the planes formed by $f$ and $g$, $f$ onto the planes formed by $g$ and $e$, and $g$ onto the planes formed by $e$ and $f$ be represented by $e_1, f_1,$ and $g_1$ respectively.\n   - We exclude cases where any of $e, f,$ or $g$ are perpendicular to the plane formed by the other two lines; such configurations would trivialize the problem.\n\n2. **Verification of Uniqueness and Existence:**\n   - Since $e, f,$ and $g$ are in different planes passing through $O$, their projections $e_1, f_1,$ and $g_1$ are unique lines intersecting $O$.\n   - Based on the definitions, these projections are distinct and lie in the respective planes formed by the combinations of $e, f,$ and $g$.\n\n3. **Construction of Geometric Elements:**\n   - From a point $E$ on $e$, drop perpendiculars to $f_1$ (in the plane of $e$ and $g$) and $g_1$ (in the plane of $e$ and $f$). \n   - Let these perpendiculars intersect $g$ at $G$ and $f$ at $F$ respectively.\n\n4. **Main Claim:**\n   - We need to show that the line segment $FG$ is perpendicular to the projection $e_1$ (which lies in the plane formed by $f$ and $g$).\n\n5. **Analysis Using Planes and Projections:**\n   - Consider the line $EG$ intersecting $f_1$ at $F_1$ and $EF$ intersecting $g_1$ at $G_1$.\n   - The plane $\\gamma$ containing $g$ and $g_1$ is perpendicular to $EF$:\n     - Since $OG_1$ (projection of $g$) is perpendicular to $EF$, it also ensures that $\\gamma$ contains a line perpendicular to $EF$ not parallel to $OG_1$, making $\\gamma$ perpendicular to $EF$.\n   - Similarly, the plane $\\varphi$ containing $f$ and $f_1$ is perpendicular to $EG$.\n\n6. **Intersection Analysis:**\n   - The lines $FF_1$ and $GG_1$ lie in the plane $EFG$ and intersect at $H$.\n   - Therefore, $OH$ (the intersection line of planes $\\varphi$ and $\\gamma$) is perpendicular to $EG$ and $EF$, and hence to the plane $EFG$.\n   - Consequently, $FG$ is also perpendicular to the plane $EFG$.\n\n7. **Plane $\\eta$ Consideration:**\n   - Suppose $OH$ intersects $FG$ at $E_1$.\n   - Then, the plane $\\eta^*$ formed by $OHE$, including line $OE = e$, is perpendicular to $FG$.\n   - Since $OE_1$ is both perpendicular to $FG$ and the plane $\\eta^*$, $\\eta^*$ is identified as the plane of the projection of $e$ onto $f$ and $g$.\n\n8. **Projection Orthogonality:**\n   - As $\\eta^*$ is equivalent to plane $\\eta$ used for the projection, the $e_1$ projection lies along $OE_1$ in $\\eta$ and is thus perpendicular to $FG$.\n\n9. **Special Cases and Final Considerations:**\n   - If $e$ is perpendicular to $g$ but not $f$:\n     - $F$ coincides with $O$ leading $FG = g$ which confirms FG is perpendicular to $e_1$.\n   \n10. **Conclusion:**\n   - Beyond the excluded cases, if $f$ and $g$ are not perpendicular and $e$ isn\u2019t perpendicular to their planes, then $FG$ is always formed and perpendicular to $e_1$.\n   - Hence, the given problem's primary justification holds with the presented geometric analysis.\n\n$\\boxed{\\text{yes}}$", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Returning from the Palace of Sports, Nina and her coach had the following dialogue:\n\n- Do you have a brother?\n- I have three brothers, - Nina replied.\n- What are their ages?\n- The product of the numbers representing their ages is 36, and the sum equals the number on that bus over there at the stop.\n\nAfter looking at the bus number, the coach said that this information was not enough for him.\n\n- The eldest one also loves sports, - added Nina.\n- Now I know how old each of them is, - said the coach and accurately named each boy's age.\n\nWhat are the ages of Nina's brothers?", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to figure out the ages of Nina's three brothers based on the conversation she had with her coach. Let me start by breaking down the information given step by step.\n\nFirst, Nina mentions that the product of their ages is 36. So, I need to list all possible combinations of three positive integers that multiply to 36. Then, she says that the sum of their ages is equal to the number on the bus, but the coach couldn't determine the ages from that sum alone. Finally, when Nina mentions that the eldest one loves sports, the coach was able to figure it out. So, the key here is that there must be some ambiguity initially because multiple sets of ages have the same sum, and the mention of an \"eldest\" brother helps eliminate that ambiguity.\n\nAlright, let's start by listing all the possible triplets of positive integers whose product is 36. Since they are ages, they must be at least 1 year old. Let me list them out systematically:\n\n1. 1, 1, 36 \u2192 Product: 1\u00d71\u00d736 = 36; Sum: 1+1+36 = 38\n2. 1, 2, 18 \u2192 Product: 1\u00d72\u00d718 = 36; Sum: 1+2+18 = 21\n3. 1, 3, 12 \u2192 Product: 1\u00d73\u00d712 = 36; Sum: 1+3+12 = 16\n4. 1, 4, 9 \u2192 Product: 1\u00d74\u00d79 = 36; Sum: 1+4+9 = 14\n5. 1, 6, 6 \u2192 Product: 1\u00d76\u00d76 = 36; Sum: 1+6+6 = 13\n6. 2, 2, 9 \u2192 Product: 2\u00d72\u00d79 = 36; Sum: 2+2+9 = 13\n7. 2, 3, 6 \u2192 Product: 2\u00d73\u00d76 = 36; Sum: 2+3+6 = 11\n8. 3, 3, 4 \u2192 Product: 3\u00d73\u00d74 = 36; Sum: 3+3+4 = 10\n\nWait, let me check if I missed any combinations. For example, starting with 1, then 2, etc. Let's see:\n\n- 1,1,36; 1,2,18; 1,3,12; 1,4,9; 1,6,6\n- Then with 2 as the first number: 2,2,9; 2,3,6\n- With 3 as the first number: 3,3,4\n- I think that's all. Let me verify that there are no other combinations. For instance, 2,2,9 and 3,3,4 are the only ones starting with 2 and 3. 4 as the first number would lead to 4,1,9 which is already covered as 1,4,9. Similarly, 6,6,1 is the same as 1,6,6. So that's all possible triplets.\n\nNow, the next part is that the sum equals the bus number, but the coach said that information wasn't enough. That implies that the sum must correspond to more than one triplet. So, we need to look for sums that are the same for multiple triplets.\n\nLooking at the sums:\n\n- 38 (unique)\n- 21 (unique)\n- 16 (unique)\n- 14 (unique)\n- 13 (appears twice: 1,6,6 and 2,2,9)\n- 11 (unique)\n- 10 (unique)\n\nSo the only sum that is not unique is 13. Therefore, the bus number must be 13 because that's the only sum that occurs more than once. Hence, the coach couldn't determine the ages because there were two possibilities: (1,6,6) and (2,2,9). \n\nThen Nina adds that the \"eldest one\" loves sports. The use of \"eldest\" implies that there is a distinct oldest brother, meaning that the triplet can't have two children of the same oldest age. Looking at the two possible triplets with sum 13:\n\n1. (1,6,6): The ages are 1, 6, 6. Here, the two older brothers are both 6, so there isn't a distinct eldest.\n2. (2,2,9): The ages are 2, 2, 9. Here, the eldest is 9, which is unique.\n\nTherefore, the mention of an eldest brother rules out the (1,6,6) case because there isn't a single eldest\u2014there are two siblings who are 6. Thus, the correct ages must be 2, 2, and 9.\n\nWait, but let me confirm that. If the triplet were (1,6,6), the eldest would be 6, but there are two of them. So when Nina refers to \"the eldest one,\" that implies there is one eldest, so the correct triplet must have a unique eldest. Therefore, it's (2,2,9), where 9 is the eldest. That makes sense. So the coach, knowing the sum was 13 and hearing that there's an eldest, deduces that the ages are 2, 2, and 9.", "deepseek_solution": "The ages of Nina's brothers are **2, 2, and 9**.\n\n**Step-by-Step Explanation:**\n\n1. **Possible Triplets:**  \n   List all triplets (a, b, c) where \\( a \\times b \\times c = 36 \\):  \n   - (1, 1, 36), sum = 38  \n   - (1, 2, 18), sum = 21  \n   - (1, 3, 12), sum = 16  \n   - (1, 4, 9), sum = 14  \n   - (1, 6, 6), sum = 13  \n   - (2, 2, 9), sum = 13  \n   - (2, 3, 6), sum = 11  \n   - (3, 3, 4), sum = 10  \n\n2. **Ambiguous Sum Identification:**  \n   The coach couldn\u2019t determine the ages from the sum alone, meaning the sum must correspond to multiple triplets. Only **sum = 13** fits this (two triplets: (1, 6, 6) and (2, 2, 9)).\n\n3. **Clarification via \"Eldest\":**  \n   Nina\u2019s mention of an \"eldest\" implies a unique oldest brother.  \n   - (1, 6, 6): No unique eldest (two 6-year-olds).  \n   - (2, 2, 9): Distinct eldest (9 years old).  \n\nThus, the ages are **2, 2, and 9**.", "ground_truth_solution": ":  \n\nTo find the ages of Nina's three brothers, we start by considering the given conditions:\n1. The product of their ages is 36.\n2. The sum of their ages is equal to the number on the bus.\n3. Nina's additional clue that the oldest brother also likes sports helps narrow down the possibilities.\n\n**Step-by-step solution:**\n\n1. **List the possible sets of ages whose product is 36:**\n\n   Let's list all combinations of three ages (integers) whose product is 36:\n   \\[\n   \\begin{align*}\n   1 \\times 36 \\times 1 &=36 &\\quad( \\text{Sum } = 1+1+36 = 38) \\\\\n   1 \\times 2 \\times 18 &= 36 &\\quad( \\text{Sum } = 1+2+18 = 21) \\\\\n   1 \\times 3 \\times 12 &= 36 &\\quad( \\text{Sum } = 1+3+12 = 16) \\\\\n   1 \\times 4 \\times 9 &=36 &\\quad( \\text{Sum } = 1+4+9 = 14) \\\\\n   1 \\times 6 \\times 6 &= 36 &\\quad( \\text{Sum } = 1+6+6 = 13) \\\\\n   2 \\times 3 \\times 6 &= 36 &\\quad( \\text{Sum } = 2+3+6 = 11) \\\\\n   2 \\times 2 \\times 9 &= 36 &\\quad( \\text{Sum } = 2+2+9 = 13) \\\\\n   3 \\times 3 \\times 4 &= 36 &\\quad( \\text{Sum } = 3+3+4 = 10) \\\\\n   \\end{align*}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Identify the sum of ages that matches the bus number:**\n\n   Given that the trainer looked at the bus number and said that he needed more information implies there are multiple 