\n   \\]\n   This simplifies to:\n   \\[\n   3a + b + 2c + d + e + 2f + g + h = 57\n   \\]\n\n9. The sum of all unique numbers from 1 to 8 is:\n   \\[\n   1 + 2 + 3 + 4 + 5 + 6 + 7 + 8 = 36\n   \\]\n\n10. Therefore, the sum of repeated variables is:\n   \\[\n   57 - 36 = 21\n   \\]\n   This means:\n   \\[\n   2a + c + f = 21 \n   \\]\n\n11. From Equation 1 and Equation 2, we consider:\n   \\[\n   2a + b + c = 19\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   a + f - b = 2\n   \\]\n\n12. Analyzing \u201c\u5c3d\u201d > \u201c\u5c71\u201d > \u201c\u529b\u201d, we set possible values, with close numbers within the permittable range:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Assume} \\; a = 6, \\; f = 5, \\; c = 4\n   \\]\n   These values give:\n   \\[\n   2(6) + f = 21 \\implies 12 + f = 21 \\implies f = 9\n   \\]\n\n13. But f is constrained at a maximum possible value of 5 under given conditions (sum of 19).\n\n14. Thus, for valid solutions, another possible trial: \n   \\[\n   c = 4, \\; f = 5, \\; b = 3, \\; d = 2; \\; h = a_{new}\n   \\]\n\n15. Then,\n   \\[\n   6 + 3 + 6 + 4 = 19\n   \\]\n\n16. Validating for Shu\u01d0 (\u6c34), we infer the calculated permutation is:\n\n   \\[\n   Shu\u01d0 = 7\n   \\]\n\n### Conclusion:\n\\[\n\\boxed{7}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "The points \\( P, Q, \\) and \\( R \\) are located on the ellipsoid given by the equation\n\n\\[ \n\\frac{x^{2}}{a^{2}} + \\frac{y^{2}}{b^{2}} + \\frac{z^{2}}{c^{2}} = 1 \n\\]\n\nwith center \\( O \\), such that the segments \\( OP, OQ, \\) and \\( OR \\) are pairwise perpendicular to each other. Show that the distance between the plane \\( PQR \\) and \\( O \\) does not depend on the choice of \\( P, Q, \\) and \\( R \\).", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, let me try to tackle this problem step by step. So, we have an ellipsoid with equation x\u00b2/a\u00b2 + y\u00b2/b\u00b2 + z\u00b2/c\u00b2 = 1, and three points P, Q, R on it such that the lines OP, OQ, OR are pairwise perpend