cubic equation. Let's see if it factors nicely. Maybe there's a rational root. By Rational Root Theorem, possible rational roots are \u00b11.\n\nLet me test t=1: 1 - 3 + 1 + 1 = 0. 1 -3 is -2, -2 +1 is -1, -1 +1 is 0. So t=1 is a root.\n\nTherefore, we can factor out (t - 1). Let's perform polynomial division or use synthetic division.\n\nUsing synthetic division with root 1:\n\nCoefficients: 1 | -3 | 1 | 1\n\nBring down the 1.\n\nMultiply by 1: 1.\n\nAdd to next coefficient: -3 + 1 = -2.\n\nMultiply by 1: -2.\n\nAdd to next coefficient: 1 + (-2) = -1.\n\nMultiply by 1: -1.\n\nAdd to last coefficient: 1 + (-1) = 0. Perfect.\n\nSo the cubic factors as (t - 1)(t\u00b2 - 2t - 1) = 0.\n\nTherefore, the roots are t=1 and t = [2 \u00b1 sqrt(4 + 4)] / 2 = [2 \u00b1 sqrt(8)] / 2 = [2 \u00b1 2\u221a2]/2 = 1 \u00b1 \u221a2.\n\nHence, the solutions for x, y, z are 1, 1 + \u221a2, and 1 - \u221a2.\n\nBut we need to check if these satisfy the original equations. Let me verify.\n\nFirst, sum: 1 + (1 + \u221a2) + (1 - \u221a2) = 1 +1 +1 +\u221a2 -\u221a2 = 3. Correct.\n\nSum of squares: 1\u00b2 + (1 + \u221a2)\u00b2 + (1 - \u221a2)\u00b2\n\nCompute each term:\n\n1\u00b2 = 1\n\n(1 + \u221a2)\u00b2 = 1 + 2\u221a2 + 2 = 3 + 2\u221a2\n\n(1 - \u221a2)\u00b2 = 1 - 2\u221a2 + 2 = 3 - 2\u221a2\n\nAdding them up: 1 + (3 + 2\u221a2) + (3 - 2\u221a2) = 1 +3 +3 +2\u221a2 -2\u221a2 = 7. Correct.\n\nSum of cubes: 1\u00b3 + (1 + \u221a2)\u00b3 + (1 - \u221a2)\u00b3\n\nCompute each term:\n\n1\u00b3 = 1\n\n(1 + \u221a2)\u00b3 = 1 + 3\u221a2 + 3*(\u221a2)^2 + (\u221a2)^3\n\nWait, maybe easier to compute step by step:\n\n(1 + \u221a2)^3 = (1 + \u221a2)*(1 + \u221a2)^2. We already know (1 + \u221a2)^2 = 3 + 2\u221a2. So multiply by (1 + \u221a2):\n\n(3 + 2\u221a2)(1 + \u221a2) = 3*1 + 3*\u221a2 + 2\u221a2*1 + 2\u221a2*\u221a2 = 3 + 3\u221a2 + 2\u221a2 + 4 = 7 + 5\u221a2\n\nSimilarly, (1 - \u221a2)^3 = (1 - \u221a2)*(1 - \u221a2)^2. (1 - \u221a2)^2 = 3 - 2\u221a2. Multiply by (1 - \u221a2):\n\n(3 - 2\