 1 = 0,\n$$\nso \\(t = 1\\) is a root. Factor the polynomial using \\(t - 1\\):\n$$\nt^3 - 3t^2 + t + 1 = (t - 1)(t^2 - 2t - 1).\n$$\n\nSolve the quadratic \\(t^2 - 2t - 1 = 0\\) using the quadratic formula:\n$$\nt = \\frac{2 \\pm \\sqrt{4 + 4}}{2}, \\\\\nt = 1 \\pm \\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nThus, the roots are:\n$$\nx = 1, \\quad y = 1 + \\sqrt{2}, \\quad z = 1 - \\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nBecause of the symmetry of the original equations, all permutations of the roots are solutions. Therefore, the complete set of solutions includes:\n$$\n(1, 1 + \\sqrt{2}, 1 - \\sqrt{2}), \\quad (1, 1 - \\sqrt{2}, 1 + \\sqrt{2}), \\\\\n(1 + \\sqrt{2}, 1, 1 - \\sqrt{2}), \\quad (1 + \\sqrt{2}, 1 - \\sqrt{2}, 1), \\\\\n(1 - \\sqrt{2}, 1, 1 + \\sqrt{2}), \\quad (1 - \\sqrt{2}, 1 + \\sqrt{2}, 1).\n$$\n\nConclusion:\n$$\n\\boxed{(1, 1 + \\sqrt{2}, 1 - \\sqrt{2}), (1, 1 - \\sqrt{2}, 1 + \\sqrt{2}), (1 + \\sqrt{2}, 1, 1 - \\sqrt{2}), (1 + \\sqrt{2}, 1 - \\sqrt{2}, 1), (1 - \\sqrt{2}, 1, 1 + \\sqrt{2}), (1 - \\sqrt{2}, 1 + \\sqrt{2}, 1)}\n$$", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Let $a, b, c$ be the lengths of the three sides of a triangle. Prove that:\n\\[\n\\frac{a^{n}}{b+c}+\\frac{b^{n}}{c+a}+\\frac{c^{n}}{a+b} \\geqslant \\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)^{n-2} \\cdot s^{n-1}\n\\]\nwhere \\( s = \\frac{1}{2}(a+b+c) \\) and \\( n \\in \\mathbf{Z}^{+} \\).", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to prove this inequality involving the sides of a triangle. Let me start by understanding the problem statement properly. We have a triangle with sides a, b, c, and we need to show that the sum of each side raised to the power n divided by the sum of the other two sides is greater than or equal to (2/3)^{n-2} times the semiperimeter s raised to the power n-1. Here, s is (a + b + c)/2, and n is a positive integer.\n\nFirst, let me write down the inequality again to make sure I have it right:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{a^{n}}{b + c} + \\frac{b^{n}}{c + a} + \\frac{c^{n}