-4, which is excluded, hence invalid. Therefore, at a=7/2, Case 2 solution is invalid, so only Case 1 solution exists.\n\nTherefore, the values of a where the equation has exactly one solution are:\n\n1. When -1 <a \u22641: one solution from Case 1.\n\n2. When a=7/2: one solution from Case 1 (since Case 2 solution is invalid).\n\nBut we need to check if there are any other values of a where two solutions coincide or one of the cases gives a solution and the other case's solution is invalid.\n\nBut we saw that when a=7/2, Case 2 gives x=-4, which is invalid, so only Case 1 solution is present.\n\nAre there any other a where one of the solutions is invalid (i.e., x=-4)?\n\nFrom earlier, we saw:\n\n- Case 1 solution x= -4 when a=-7/2, but a=-7/2 is in a < -1, which gives no solutions.\n\n- Case 2 solution x=-4 when a=7/2, which is a >1, leading to one solution.\n\nTherefore, the only special case is a=7/2 where one of the potential solutions is invalid, resulting in exactly one solution.\n\nTherefore, the equation has exactly one solution when:\n\n- \\( -1 < a \\leq 1 \\) or \\( a = \\frac{7}{2} \\)\n\nBut let's verify with a=7/2 and a=1.\n\n**Verification for a=7/2 (3.5):**\n\nEquation: (7/2)|2 +x| + (x^2 +x -12)/(x +4)=0\n\nSimplify: (7/2)|2 +x| + x -3=0\n\nSo, (7/2)|2 +x| = 3 -x\n\nCase 1: x \u2265-2:\n\n(7/2)(2 +x) = 3 -x\n\nMultiply both sides by 2:\n\n7(2 +x) = 6 -2x\n\n14 +7x =6 -2x\n\n9x = -8\n\nx= -8/9 \u2248 -0.89, which is \u2265-2, valid.\n\nCase 2: x < -2:\n\n(7/2)(-2 -x) =3 -x\n\nMultiply by 2:\n\n-7(2 +x) =6 -2x\n\n-14 -7x=6 -2x\n\n-5x=20\n\nx= -4, which is invalid.\n\nThus, only one solution at x=-8/9. Correct.\n\n**Verification for a=1:**\n\nEquation: |2 +x| + x -3 =0\n\nCase 1: x \u2265-2:\n\n2 +x +x -3=0 \u21922x -1=0\u2192x=1/2. Valid.\n\nCase 2: x < -2:\n\n-2 -x +x -3=0\u2192-5=0, impossible.\n\nThus, only one solution. Correct.\n\n**Verification for a=0 (which is in -1 <a \u22641):**\n\nEquation: 0*|2 +x| + x -3=0\u2192x -3=0\u2192x=3. Check x=3\u